  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "Graph requires this callback even for a storage-only consumer. The\n delegation test must use only node_path; discovering edges would be\n an unwanted graph access, which this guard rejects. Traversal fixtures\n separately exercise edge discovery."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/storage.rs#impl[StoredGraph/Graph]/fn[outgoing_edges]` is a valid coverage exception.\n\n- **Role and form.** The citation is a fenced `cite` with commentary on an explicit function that has a body. That is the form `habeas:coverage` allows.\n- **The reason is substantive.** `Graph` requires `outgoing_edges`, so the storage-only `StoredGraph` fixture has to implement it. The fixture's job is to prove that storage delegation uses only `node_path`. That is why the body is a deliberate `panic!`: it fails any test that discovers edges. The panic should never run, so the function's zero execution is expected. The reason is not mere difficulty.\n- **It agrees with the current source.** The delegation test, `consumer_delegates_evidence_storage_using_only_the_requested_nodes_path`, and the `StoredGraph` `EvidenceStorage` methods only call `self.0.load` and `self.0.store` through `node_path`. They never ask for edges.\n- **The reliance on other fixtures holds.** The commentary says traversal fixtures exercise edge discovery. Real `outgoing_edges` implementations exist in `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/agent.rs`, `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/contracts.rs` and `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/validation.rs`, and they return or fail on edges.\n- **Scope.** The exception covers only this function, which is what the citation applies to.\n\nI found no gap.",
